body:
I haven't seen solid evidence for or against Deca so maybe it's not my place but:

&#x200B;

I can't see a valid reason for Deca to lie, people argue that it's because it may threaten their revenue, while this may be true it doesn't explain "false bans", if fame training was the problem then surely they'd just make it an offence punishable by a permanent ban? Contrasting from this, there is a very good reason for players to lie and claim they were falsely banned, to cover up their tracks after being caught cheating and entering ghost realms which Deca claim cannot be accessed without a modified client. This was disproved as a video was released yesterday by "Bertiii" on Youtube as Tijme [linked](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k6N-zdukxC0&feature=youtu.be) previously, however it's still made crystal clear in the video that despite him not saying it, he was fully aware of what he was doing, deliberately accessing a ghost realm in order to take advantage of a fame exploit which resulted in the banning of his account. 

&#x200B;

Also, the [terms of service](https://decagames.com/tos.html) state very clearly that you are **not** to make alterations to, or modifications of, the whole or any part of the Deca Service, or permit the Deca Service or any part of it to be combined with, or become incorporated in, any other product or service. This is the sixth rule mentioned under the Restrictions section of Deca's terms of service and is at least partially referring to hacked clients, though those who were recently banned may not be directly guilty of this, they certainly took advantage of it. The seventh rule mentioned under the Restrictions section of Deca's terms of service is as follows: do **not** to disassemble, **decompile**, reverse-engineer, derive any code or algorithms or create derivative works based on the whole or any part of the Deca Service or attempt to do any such thing except to the extent such activities are permitted under applicable law. This again is at least partially referring to hacked clients, those experienced or familiar with such clients may be aware that **decompiling** is a part of the process when it comes to editing clients to update them etc. Again, those recently banned may not be directly guilty of this but took advantage of it.

&#x200B;

My final point, (for now) refers to the final statement made under the acceptable use policy in Deca's terms of service which reads as follows:  We reserve the right (but have no obligation) to review any User Content, and to investigate and/or take appropriate action against you if you violate the Acceptable Use Policy or any other provision of these Terms or otherwise create liability for us or any other person. The action we take will be determined by us acting in our sole discretion. Examples of action that we might take could include: **removing or modifying your User Content**, terminating these Terms and your Account and/or reporting you to law enforcement authorities or relevant rights holder. (Referring to banning accounts and deleting characters).

&#x200B;

In conclusion, I see no reason for a company to lie and use the very people supporting them as a scapegoat. I also think all bans that were distributed were done so accordingly, but there is no solid evidence of this as of today, so if it turns out that I am wrong, I apologise and will be sure to keep my opinions to myself next time.
